### Impact

Heap use-after-free in the core KV engine. On the pooled worker: reads of freed transaction fields and of a freed raw RocksDB snapshot pointer; a heap **write** when the pooled closure drops the `MutexGuard` (unlock of a freed mutex). On the cursor paths the worker also writes into freed cursor state and can invoke `&mut dyn KeyVisitor/ValVisitor` callbacks through freed state. Affected backends: `kv-rocksdb`, `kv-mem`, `kv-surrealkv`. Consequence class: in-process memory corruption (crash, data disclosure, potential code execution in the host process) for Rust embedders of `surrealdb-core` that leak a dispatched future.

Preconditions (honest scope): the caller must leak the future once; we found no existing leak site in the tree (the PoC performs the leak explicitly), and we could not make it remote/black-box triggerable - no network/SurrealQL/JS/WASM input can call `mem::forget`. Provisional scoring: `CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:H/PR:L/UI:N/S:C/C:H/I:H/A:H` (High, 7.1) as an embedder-facing defect; please re-score as you see fit. Weakness: CWE-416 (primary), CWE-672 (secondary).

Suggested remediation: stop making a destructor load-bearing for memory safety - have the pool closures own their state (e.g. `Arc`-based transaction state and owned `ReadOptions`, or re-acquire state inside the closure) so a leaked future can at most leak memory; short term, upgrade to the `affinitypool` release that makes `spawn_local` `unsafe` and wrap the call sites in documented `unsafe` blocks with one regression test each.
